<section class="grid2" aria-label="Overview and links">
  <div class="card">
    <h2 class="section-title">Why a dictionary is “core”</h2>
    <p class="muted">
      Most reporting failures aren’t “dashboard problems” — they’re <strong>definition drift</strong>:
      different teams calculate the “same” metric differently (filters, grain, time rules, data source fields),
      and the organization loses trust in the numbers.
    </p>

    <div class="miniGrid" aria-label="Principles">
      <div class="mini">
        <b>Consistency</b>
        <span>One metric name → one definition → one formula. No local interpretations.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="mini">
        <b>Auditability</b>
        <span>Every metric documents grain, filters, data source fields, and refresh cadence.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="mini">
        <b>Actionability</b>
        <span>Each metric includes interpretation notes to connect signals to decisions.</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Design intent:</strong> the dictionary is tool-agnostic.
      It can power dashboards, scorecards, weekly business reviews, and A[i]gent decision logic.
    </div>
  </div>

  <aside class="card">
    <h2 class="section-title">Related</h2>
    <p class="small">
      The dictionary is the contract between data, analytics, and operators. Tools can render and apply it,
      but the dictionary itself is the governance layer.
